    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nWhen pushing at an angle $\\theta$ below horizontal, the normal reaction increases: $N = Mg + F\\sin\\theta$. The limiting friction increases as $F$ increases: $f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s(Mg + F\\sin\\theta)$. Motion requires $F\\cos\\theta > \\mu_s Mg + \\mu_s F\\sin\\theta \\implies F(\\cos\\theta - \\mu_s\\sin\\theta) > \\mu_s Mg$. If $\\cos\\theta \\le \\mu_s\\sin\\theta$, this condition can never be met for any $F > 0$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nPushing force $F$ inclined at $\\theta$ below horizontal, coefficient $\\mu_s$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nCondition for motion: $F(\\cos\\theta - \\mu_s\\sin\\theta) > \\mu_s Mg$. Self-locking condition: $\\cos\\theta \\le \\mu_s\\sin\\theta \\implies \\tan\\theta \\ge \\frac{1}{\\mu_s}$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Resolve pushing force $F$:\n   - Horizontal component pushing forward: $F_x = F\\cos\\theta$.\n   - Vertical downward component: $F_y = F\\sin\\theta$.\n2. The normal reaction becomes: $N = Mg + F\\sin\\theta$.\n3. The limiting static friction opposing motion is: $f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s N = \\mu_s Mg + \\mu_s F\\sin\\theta$.\n4. For sliding to occur, the forward force must exceed limiting friction: $F\\cos\\theta > \\mu_s Mg + \\mu_s F\\sin\\theta$.\n5. Rearranging: $F(\\cos\\theta - \\mu_s\\sin\\theta) > \\mu_s Mg$.\n6. If $\\cos\\theta - \\mu_s\\sin\\theta \\le 0$, the left-hand side is negative or zero, while the right-hand side is positive ($+ \\mu_s Mg$).\n7. Therefore, no matter how immense $F$ is made, $F_x$ can never overcome $f_{\\text{lim}}$. The block is locked in place!\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: Cannot be moved horizontally, no matter how large $F$ is (self-locking)\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nThis phenomenon is called self-locking or jamming! It explains why pushing a lawn roller at a steep angle jams it into the turf.",
