    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nOnce relative motion begins, the microscopic contact asperities do not have sufficient time to form deep intermolecular cold welds. Consequently, kinetic friction is slightly less than limiting static friction ($f_k < f_{\\text{lim}}$), meaning $\\mu_k < \\mu_s$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nProperties of kinetic friction.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$f_k = \\mu_k N < \\mu_s N = f_{\\text{lim}}$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Intermolecular cold-welds require contact time to bond fully.\n2. In static contact, asperities settle into one another, creating strong bonds.\n3. During sliding, asperities shear across each other rapidly without having time to interlock as deeply.\n4. Hence, the force needed to maintain motion ($f_k$) is less than the force needed to initiate motion ($f_{\\text{lim}}$).\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: Kinetic friction is slightly less than limiting static friction\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\n$\\mu_k < \\mu_s$ always for any pair of dry surfaces! It is always easier to keep a body sliding than to start it sliding.",
