    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nThe coefficient of friction $\\mu$ is an intrinsic property of the materials in contact and their surface condition. It is independent of the normal force. It is the frictional force $f = \\mu N$ that triples, not $\\mu$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nNormal force tripled from $N$ to $3N$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$f = \\mu N$; $\\mu$ is constant.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. $\\mu$ is a material property determined by atomic composition and surface roughness.\n2. When normal force $N$ increases to $3N$, the limiting friction increases to $f' = \\mu(3N) = 3f$.\n3. The ratio $\\frac{f'}{N'} = \\frac{3f}{3N} = \\mu$ remains constant.\n4. Therefore, the coefficient of friction $\\mu$ does not change.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: It remains unchanged\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nNEET Trap: Don't confuse the frictional force ($f$) with the coefficient of friction ($\\mu$)! $f$ triples, but $\\mu$ stays constant.",
