    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nAt the verge of slipping down an inclined plane, the component of gravity down the incline ($mg\\sin\\theta$) equals the maximum static (limiting) friction ($f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s N = \\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta$). Dividing gives $\\tan\\theta = \\mu_s \\implies \\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\mu_s)$. This critical angle is known as the angle of repose.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nMass $m$, inclined plane at angle $\\theta$, coefficient $\\mu_s$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$mg\\sin\\theta = \\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta \\implies \\tan\\theta = \\mu_s \\implies \\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\mu_s)$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Weight acts vertically downward with magnitude $mg$.\n2. Resolving parallel and perpendicular to the inclined plane:\n   - Component down the plane: $F_\\parallel = mg\\sin\\theta$.\n   - Component into the plane: $F_\\perp = mg\\cos\\theta$.\n3. Normal reaction: $N = mg\\cos\\theta$.\n4. Limiting static friction acting up the plane: $f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s N = \\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta$.\n5. At the verge of slipping: $mg\\sin\\theta = f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta$.\n6. Divide by $mg\\cos\\theta$: $\\tan\\theta = \\mu_s \\implies \\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\mu_s)$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}(\\mu_s)$\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nAngle of repose $\\theta_r = \\tan^{-1}(\\mu_s)$. It equals the angle of friction $\\lambda = \\tan^{-1}(\\mu_s)$!",
