    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nWork-Energy Theorem: The work done by friction $-f_k \\cdot s$ must absorb the entire initial kinetic energy $K$: $W_{\\text{friction}} = \\Delta K \\implies f_k s = K \\implies s = \\frac{K}{f_k} = \\frac{K}{\\mu_k mg}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nKinetic energy $K$, friction force $f_k = \\mu_k mg$, final $K_f = 0$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$W_{\\text{friction}} = f_k s = K \\implies s = \\frac{K}{\\mu_k mg}$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. By the work-energy theorem, the net work done on the block equals its change in kinetic energy: $W_{\\text{net}} = K_f - K_i$.\n2. Here, $K_f = 0$ and $K_i = K$.\n3. The only horizontal force doing work is kinetic friction $f_k = \\mu_k mg$ acting opposite to displacement: $W = -f_k s$.\n4. Equating: $-f_k s = 0 - K \\implies f_k s = K$.\n5. Solving for distance $s$: $s = \\frac{K}{f_k} = \\frac{K}{\\mu_k mg}$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $\\frac{K}{\\mu_k mg}$\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nWork done by friction equals initial kinetic energy: $f_k s = K \\implies s = \\frac{K}{\\mu_k mg}$!",
