    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nWork-Energy Theorem with variable friction: $W = -\\int_0^{x_f} f(x) dx = -\\int_0^{x_f} \\mu(x) mg dx = -mg k \\int_0^{x_f} x dx = -\\frac{1}{2}mg k x_f^2$. Setting work equal to $\\Delta K = 0 - \\frac{1}{2}mv_0^2$ gives $\\frac{1}{2}mg k x_f^2 = \\frac{1}{2}mv_0^2 \\implies x_f = \\sqrt{\\frac{v_0^2}{kg}}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\n$\\mu(x) = kx$, initial velocity $v_0$ at $x = 0$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$\\Delta K = W_{\\text{friction}} \\implies \\frac{1}{2}mv_0^2 = \\int_0^{x_f} kx mg dx = \\frac{1}{2}mg k x_f^2 \\implies x_f = \\sqrt{\\frac{v_0^2}{kg}}$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Frictional force at position $x$ is $f(x) = \\mu(x) mg = (kx) mg$.\n2. Work done by friction as the body moves from $x = 0$ to $x_f$:\n   $W = -\\int_0^{x_f} f(x) dx = -mg k \\int_0^{x_f} x dx = -\\frac{1}{2}mg k x_f^2$.\n3. By the work-energy theorem: $W = K_f - K_i = 0 - \\frac{1}{2}mv_0^2$.\n4. Equating magnitudes: $\\frac{1}{2}mg k x_f^2 = \\frac{1}{2}mv_0^2$.\n5. Cancelling $\\frac{1}{2}m$: $g k x_f^2 = v_0^2 \\implies x_f^2 = \\frac{v_0^2}{kg}$.\n6. Taking square root: $x_f = \\sqrt{\\frac{v_0^2}{kg}} = \\frac{v_0}{\\sqrt{kg}}$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $\\sqrt{\\frac{v_0^2}{kg}}$\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nWhenever friction varies as $\\mu = kx$, integrate work: $\\int kx mg dx = \\frac{1}{2}kmg x^2$. Stopping distance is $x_f = \\frac{v_0}{\\sqrt{kg}}$!",
