    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nFor a HORIZONTAL pushing force $F$ on an incline: Force parallel to incline pointing up is $F\\cos\\theta$. Force perpendicular pushing into incline is $F\\sin\\theta$. Normal force is $N = mg\\cos\\theta + F\\sin\\theta$. Limiting friction acts up the plane: $f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s(mg\\cos\\theta + F\\sin\\theta)$. Impending slide down requires: $F\\cos\\theta + f_{\\text{lim}} = mg\\sin\\theta \\implies F = mg \\frac{\\sin\\theta - \\mu_s\\cos\\theta}{\\cos\\theta + \\mu_s\\sin\\theta}$. For $\\theta = 45^\\circ$, $F = mg \\frac{1 - \\mu_s}{1 + \\mu_s}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\n$\\theta = 45^\\circ$, $\\mu_s = 0.5$, horizontal force $F$, preventing downward sliding.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$F_{\\text{min}} = mg \\frac{\\sin\\theta - \\mu_s\\cos\\theta}{\\cos\\theta + \\mu_s\\sin\\theta} = mg \\frac{1 - \\mu_s}{1 + \\mu_s}$ for $\\theta = 45^\\circ$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Resolve forces along and perpendicular to the inclined plane:\n   - Gravity: $mg\\sin 45^\\circ$ down the plane, $mg\\cos 45^\\circ$ into the plane.\n   - Horizontal force $F$: $F\\cos 45^\\circ$ up the plane, $F\\sin 45^\\circ$ into the plane.\n2. Normal reaction: $N = mg\\cos 45^\\circ + F\\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(mg + F)$.\n3. Because the block tends to slide down, limiting static friction acts UP the plane:\n   $f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s N = \\frac{\\mu_s}{\\sqrt{2}}(mg + F)$.\n4. Upward forces along incline balance downward component of gravity:\n   $F\\cos 45^\\circ + f_{\\text{lim}} = mg\\sin 45^\\circ$.\n5. $\\frac{F}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{\\mu_s}{\\sqrt{2}}(mg + F) = \\frac{mg}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\n6. Multiplying by $\\sqrt{2}$: $F(1 + \\mu_s) + \\mu_s mg = mg \\implies F(1 + \\mu_s) = mg(1 - \\mu_s)$.\n7. Therefore, $F = mg \\frac{1 - \\mu_s}{1 + \\mu_s}$.\n8. Substitute $\\mu_s = 0.5$: $F = mg \\frac{1 - 0.5}{1 + 0.5} = mg \\frac{0.5}{1.5} = \\frac{1}{3}mg \\approx 0.33 mg$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $\\frac{1}{3} mg \\approx 0.33 mg$\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nAt $45^\\circ$, minimum horizontal force to prevent slip is $F = mg\\frac{1 - \\mu_s}{1 + \\mu_s}$. Minimum horizontal force to push up is $F = mg\\frac{1 + \\mu_s}{1 - \\mu_s}$!",
