    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nTo push up the plane, friction acts DOWN the plane: $F\\cos\\theta = mg\\sin\\theta + \\mu_s(mg\\cos\\theta + F\\sin\\theta) \\implies F = mg \\frac{\\sin\\theta + \\mu_s\\cos\\theta}{\\cos\\theta - \\mu_s\\sin\\theta}$. For $\\theta = 45^\\circ$, $F = mg \\frac{1 + \\mu_s}{1 - \\mu_s}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\n$\\theta = 45^\\circ$, $\\mu_s = 0.5$, pushing up the incline.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$F_{\\text{up}} = mg \\frac{1 + \\mu_s}{1 - \\mu_s} = mg \\frac{1 + 0.5}{1 - 0.5} = mg \\frac{1.5}{0.5} = 3 mg$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. When the block is on the verge of moving UP the incline, friction acts DOWN the plane.\n2. Upward force along incline is $F\\cos 45^\\circ$.\n3. Downward forces along incline: gravity component $mg\\sin 45^\\circ$ and limiting friction $f_{\\text{lim}} = \\mu_s N$.\n4. Normal force is $N = mg\\cos 45^\\circ + F\\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(mg + F)$.\n5. Equilibrium equation:\n   $F\\cos 45^\\circ = mg\\sin 45^\\circ + \\mu_s N \\implies \\frac{F}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{mg}{\\sqrt{2}} + \\frac{\\mu_s}{\\sqrt{2}}(mg + F)$.\n6. Multiplying by $\\sqrt{2}$: $F - \\mu_s F = mg + \\mu_s mg \\implies F(1 - \\mu_s) = mg(1 + \\mu_s)$.\n7. Therefore, $F = mg \\frac{1 + \\mu_s}{1 - \\mu_s}$.\n8. With $\\mu_s = 0.5$: $F = mg \\frac{1.5}{0.5} = 3 mg$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $3 mg$\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nNotice the massive contrast: To PREVENT sliding down requires only $0.33 mg$, but to PUSH UP requires $3 mg$ (9 times as much force)!",
