    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nFor the block not to slide down the smooth inclined face, the wedge must accelerate with acceleration $a$ such that the pseudo force $ma\\cos\\theta$ up the incline balances the gravity component $mg\\sin\\theta$ down the incline: $ma\\cos\\theta = mg\\sin\\theta \\implies a = g\\tan\\theta$. The external horizontal force accelerating the combined system of mass $(M + m)$ is $F = (M + m) a = (M + m) g \\tan\\theta$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nWedge mass $M$, block mass $m$, smooth surfaces, inclination $\\theta$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n$a = g\\tan\\theta$; $F = (M + m) a = (M + m) g \\tan\\theta$.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. In the frame of reference of the accelerating wedge (acceleration $a$ to the left):\n   - Block experiences a backward pseudo force $ma$ directed to the right horizontally.\n   - Component of pseudo force up the incline: $F_{\\text{pseudo}, \\parallel} = ma\\cos\\theta$.\n   - Component of gravity down the incline: $F_{g, \\parallel} = mg\\sin\\theta$.\n2. For the block to remain stationary relative to the wedge, these two opposing components along the incline must balance:\n   $ma\\cos\\theta = mg\\sin\\theta \\implies a = g\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = g\\tan\\theta$.\n3. Now consider the entire system of total mass $(M + m)$ in the ground frame:\n   - Both masses accelerate together horizontally at $a = g\\tan\\theta$.\n   - The floor is smooth, so no horizontal external friction acts.\n4. Therefore, the required applied force is:\n   $F = (M + m) a = (M + m) g \\tan\\theta$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $(M + m) g \\tan\\theta$\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nUniversal NEET Benchmark Question: Required acceleration is $a = g\\tan\\theta$, and required external force is $F = (M + m)g\\tan\\theta$!",
